\title{Univariate polynomials with vector coefficients}
\taxon{Definition}
\tag{definition}
\meta{defines}{vector-valued univariate polynomial; polynomial with vector coefficients; coefficient tuple; polynomial coefficient matrix}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{Notation used in [OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), proof of Lemma 2.1; explanatory definition added on 9 October 2026.}

\p{Let #{W} be a finite-dimensional real vector space, the space of coefficient vectors. A \em{univariate polynomial with coefficients in #{W}} is a finite formal sum}

##{P(\xi)=\sum_{r=0}^{h}w_r\xi^r,\qquad w_r\in W.}

\p{There is one indeterminate, #{\xi}. The notation #{W[\xi]} denotes the space of all such polynomials. For #{P\ne0}, its degree is the largest #{r} with #{w_r\ne0}; the zero polynomial is included in every degree bound. For a fixed bound #{h\geq0}, the \em{coefficient tuple} gives the linear bijection}

##{\begin{aligned}
W^{h+1}&\longrightarrow W[\xi]_{\leq h},\\
(w_0,\ldots,w_h)&\longmapsto\sum_{r=0}^{h}w_r\xi^r.
\end{aligned}}

\p{Tuple slots are numbered #{0,\ldots,h}: slot #{r} contains the vector coefficient of the monomial #{\xi^r}. This is the correspondence #{r\leftrightarrow\xi^r} between slots and monomial degrees. Evaluation at a scalar #{\zeta\in\mathbb R} gives #{P(\zeta)=\sum_{r=0}^{h}\zeta^r w_r\in W}.}

\p{To express each coefficient vector in scalar coordinates, choose a basis #{e_1,\ldots,e_N} of #{W} and write #{w_r=\sum_{j=1}^{N}c_{jr}e_j}. The \em{polynomial coefficient matrix} #{C=(c_{jr})} has #{N} rows and #{h+1} columns. Row #{j} records the #{e_j}-coordinate in #{W}; column #{r}, numbered from zero, records the coefficient of #{\xi^r}. Equivalently, the scalar coordinates of the whole polynomial correspond to the basis elements #{e_j\xi^r}, indexed by pairs #{(j,r)}.}

##{[P(\zeta)]_{(e_1,\ldots,e_N)}=C\begin{pmatrix}1\\\zeta\\\vdots\\\zeta^h\end{pmatrix}.}

\p{Thus #{N=\dim W} counts coordinates of each vector coefficient, while #{h+1} counts monomial slots. These are independent parameters. For a subspace #{Z\subseteq W}, the notation #{Z[\xi]} imposes #{w_r\in Z} for every #{r}; its degree-at-most-#{h} part corresponds to #{Z^{h+1}}.}

\p{For example, with #{W=\mathbb R^2},}

##{P(\xi)=(1,3)+\xi(2,0)+\xi^2(0,-1)=(1+2\xi,\,3-\xi^2).}

\p{Its coefficient matrix has columns corresponding, in order, to #{1,\xi,\xi^2}:}

##{C=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&0\\3&0&-1\end{pmatrix}.}

\p{This is a degree-two polynomial in one variable, with values in #{\mathbb R^2}; for instance #{P(1)=(3,2)}.}
